Generated by: Andrew Teylu <andrew@tey.lu>
Generated on: 2026-08-24
Generator: Model-driven synthetic Latendresse profile generator, with empirical structure extracted from the bundled canonical biological model.
Application: Synthetic linear-real and floating-point benchmarks modeled on the structure of the existing SMT-LIB QF_LRA Latendresse benchmarks [1], which originate in flux-balance-analysis and pathway-database model-completion applications [2].
Biological inspiration: E. coli growth on glycerol with discrete objective, nutrient, and secretion choices, specifically the ecoliMILPglycerolYices3 model in the existing SMT-LIB Latendresse family.
Target solver: cvc5
Time limit: 300

The model has 100 real-valued reaction variables, 3 real-valued 0/1 selectors (1 objective, 1 nutrient, and 1 secretion), and 58 exact mass-balance rows spanning 1 to 44 reactions. Every selector has a paired reaction with the two inequalities used by the canonical glycerol model. The objective preserves the canonical +10000 selector rewards, nutrient/secretion costs, -1/10 background flux cost, and objective-reaction coefficient ratios under one positive size-dependent normalization.

Objective regime: beyond. The mixed glycerol objective is lower-bounded at one eighth above the independently attainable maximum, making the QF_LRA instance unsatisfiable.
Numeric profile: native. Flux bounds use 1000, and row coefficients are sampled from the canonical family model.

This file is a structural floating-point translation of that source instance. Real variables are replaced by (_ FloatingPoint 5 11) variables. Real arithmetic is replaced by roundNearestTiesToEven floating-point arithmetic, and numeric constants are pre-rounded once to exact target-format literals. Linear comparisons become floating-point comparisons; equality uses IEEE fp.eq. No finite-value guards are added.

Floating point is not the reals; satisfiability is not assumed to survive this translation. The translated status is unknown.

[1] Timothy King, Clark Barrett, and Bruno Dutertre, Simplex with Sum of Infeasibilities for SMT, FMCAD 2013.
[2] Mario Latendresse, Markus Krummenacker, Miles Trupp, and Peter D. Karp, Construction and completion of flux balance models from pathway databases, Bioinformatics 28(3), 388-396, 2012.
|)
